Scenario ID: S03
Scenario title: Wafer Sample Transfer and Disposal Request
Initial workflow description:
Samples are moved when they are needed for evaluation. After evaluation, unnecessary samples are checked and disposed of through a request process.

Domain expert answers:
{
  "answers": [
    {
      "question_id": "Q1",
      "question": "Who is responsible for initiating a sample transfer request — is it the engineer requesting the evaluation, a dedicated lab technician, or is the transfer triggered automatically by a system event?",
      "answer": "The sample requester initiates the transfer. They first identify the required sample and evaluation purpose, then submit the transfer request form. It is a manual, role-driven action, not an automatic system trigger, though automation could pre-fill request fields from sample identifiers and evaluation purpose."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "What specific criteria or conditions determine that a sample is 'unnecessary' after evaluation and should be flagged for disposal — for example, are there defined evaluation outcomes, age thresholds, or quantity rules?",
      "answer": "The key condition is whether disposal is allowed after evaluation — specifically, whether a retention requirement applies. If no retention condition is met, the sample becomes a disposal candidate. The verification engineer updates sample status post-evaluation and then submits the disposal request. Specific age thresholds or quantity rules are not specified in the workflow."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "Does the disposal request require approval from any role before it can be executed, and if so, what is the approval authority and what conditions might lead to a rejection?",
      "answer": "Yes, an approver must approve and record the disposal before it is executed. A rejection would occur if a retention requirement applies to the sample — this is an explicit exception in the workflow. Other conditions for rejection are not specified beyond the retention rule."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What system or tool is currently used to track sample status, location, and evaluation results throughout this workflow — for example, a laboratory information management system, a spreadsheet, or another platform?",
      "answer": "The workflow uses a sample tracking sheet to monitor sample status, a transfer request form for initiating moves, a storage location registry for location tracking, and an approval system for managing approvals. These appear to be distinct tools rather than a single integrated platform."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "What is the expected output at the end of the disposal request process — for example, is a disposal record, confirmation log, or compliance certificate generated, and where is it stored?",
      "answer": "The end of the disposal process produces a disposal record and an updated sample status. A physical confirmation note is also an output of the broader workflow. Where these records are stored is not specified beyond the systems listed — sample tracking sheet and approval system."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Approximately how many sample transfers and disposal requests occur per week or month, and are they handled individually on demand or grouped into scheduled batches?",
      "answer": "The workflow does not specify volume figures such as transfers or disposals per week or month. Based on the workflow structure, requests appear to be handled individually on demand rather than in scheduled batches, though this is not explicitly stated."
    }
  ]
}
